Method poll() returns E. Implementation: final Node<E> f = first; return (f == null) ? null : unlinkFirst(f);

So behavior: if first is null (list empty) returns null. If not empty, then f != null, returns unlinkFirst(f). What does unlinkFirst return? Not given, but typical LinkedList unlinkFirst returns element of node f (item) after removing it. So result equals f.item (initial first element). However comment includes case where result is null and the first element was initially null. That is possible if first node's item is null; unlinkFirst returns null. So the spec: If initially not empty (i.e., \old(this.size()) > 0), then either result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null, or result == \old(this.getFirst()).

But need to express using observers: we have methods: size(), getFirst(), maybe peekFirst()? But getFirst throws NoSuchElementException if empty, but we can still call it when not empty. However spec may use this.peekFirst() which returns null if empty. But we need initial value before operation. Use \old(this.getFirst())? Since precondition: list not empty, getFirst() is defined. Use \old(this.getFirst()).

Spec: \old(this.size()) > 0 => ( \result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null ) || \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst()))? Wait result may be null, but equality check with null using ==. For non-null values, we can use == as they are objects; but we can use \result == \old(this.getFirst()) . However if both null, that also satisfies equality, but comment splits case. The spec: either result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null, or result == \old(this.getFirst()). Actually second case includes null case too, but they want distinct. Use ( \result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null ) || (\result == \old(this.getFirst())) . This includes duplicate but fine.

Better: \old(this.size()) > 0 => ( (\result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null) || \result == \old(this.getFirst()) );

But note that if initial first element is null, then result could be null, which satisfies both conditions, but that's okay.

We need to express size before: \old(this.size()) > 0.

Thus final assert: assert \old(this.size()) > 0 => ( (\result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null) || \result == \old(this.getFirst()) );

Make sure parentheses correct.

Return type is E, may be generic; equality using == is okay for reference equality. Could also use Objects.equals but not available. Use ==.

